Thanks — I can help. Before I compute CAC and LTV precisely, two quick items I need to confirm, because they materially affect the finance narrative:

1) Cash / runway inconsistency
- You said Riverfront has $1.5M cash and 6 months runway, but you also said burn is $72K/month. 1.5M / 72K ≈ 20.8 months, not 6 months. If runway is actually 6 months, burn must be ≈ $250K/month. Please confirm which numbers are correct (cash, runway, burn).

2) Missing inputs for CAC and LTV
To calculate CAC and LTV I need any of the following (the more you give, the more accurate):
- Acquisition spend (marketing + sales) over a period (e.g., last quarter or last 12 months)
- Number of new customers acquired in that same period
- Number of active customers (or customers at period start and end)
- Average revenue per customer (ARPU) over the same period, or revenue split by customer cohort
- Gross margin (contribution margin) % — LTV should use gross margin, not revenue
- Churn rate or average customer lifetime (months or years)
- Whether revenue is recurring (SaaS/subscription) or transactional (one-off orders)

If you don’t have all of those, I can still produce scenario-based estimates — below I show formulas, a labeled worked example using conservative assumptions, and the finance narrative language you can use.

Formulas (simple, standard)
- CAC = Total acquisition spend (marketing + sales) / Number of new customers acquired
- LTV (cohort) = ARPU per period × Gross margin % × Customer lifetime (in same periods)
  OR equivalently LTV = (ARPU per period × Gross margin %) / Churn rate (if churn is periodic)
- LTV : CAC ratio = LTV / CAC — rule of thumb: >3:1 attractive; <1 unsustainable
- CAC payback period = CAC / (monthly gross margin per customer)

Worked example (clear assumptions)
Assumptions (example only — I will replace with real inputs if you provide them):
- Revenue last quarter = $1.8M → monthly revenue = $600K
- Active customers = 120 (so ARPU per month = 600K / 120 = $5,000)
- Acquisition spend last quarter = $180K
- New customers last quarter = 30 → CAC = 180K / 30 = $6,000 per new customer
- Gross margin = 40% (typical for asset-heavy logistics could vary)
- Monthly churn = 2% → Average lifetime ≈ 1 / 0.02 = 50 months

Calculations (based on these assumptions)
- ARPU monthly = $5,000
- Monthly gross margin per customer = 5,000 × 40% = $2,000
- LTV = monthly gross margin × avg lifetime = 2,000 × 50 = $100,000
- LTV : CAC = 100,000 / 6,000 ≈ 16.7 : 1 (very healthy)
- CAC payback period = CAC / monthly gross margin = 6,000 / 2,000 = 3 months

Notes on this example: it shows strong economics, but results are extremely sensitive to churn and gross margin. For instance, if churn were 5%/month (avg lifetime 20 months) and gross margin 25%, LTV drops dramatically.

Recommended data checklist (for a precise model)
- Quarterly and trailing-12 acquisition spend (breakdown: channel, paid ads, sales commissions)
- New customers acquired per period and definition of customer (signed contract? first order?)
- ARPU by cohort or segment (enterprise vs SMB)
- Gross margin by customer segment (include direct costs)
- Churn/retention by cohort and monthly/annual basis
- Lifetime assumptions and billing frequency
- Any one-time onboarding or implementation costs per customer

Finance narrative bullets you can use in internal materials
- “Current liquidity: $1.5M cash. Please confirm burn — we’re seeing either 6 months runway (implies ~ $250K/month burn) or 20.8 months runway if burn is $72K/month. Clarifying this is critical to board messaging.”
- “CAC (definition): total marketing + sales spend to acquire one customer. We measure CAC per quarter and by channel to prioritize high-return channels.”
- “LTV (definition): expected gross-margin contribution from a typical customer over their lifetime. We use gross margin, not revenue, to compute LTV.”
- “Target metrics: LTV:CAC ≥ 3 and CAC payback under 12 months for growth-stage durability. If payback >12 months, prioritize reducing CAC or increasing gross margins/ARPU.”
- “Key levers: improve onboarding to reduce churn, upsell and cross-sell to increase ARPU, and shift spend to lower-CAC channels (organic, partnerships).”
- “Immediate ask: provide acquisition spend + new customers last 4 quarters and gross margin assumptions so we can produce exact CAC, LTV, payback, and a sensitivity table for the board.”

Offer
- I can compute exact CAC & LTV and produce a one-page summary + a 2-slide deck if you give me:
  - acquisition spend and new customers (last quarter and trailing 12 months)
  - gross margin %
  - churn rate or avg lifetime, and whether revenue is recurring
- Or, if you prefer, I can run sensitivity scenarios now using plausible ranges (low/medium/high churn, different gross margins) and return a table and suggested narrative.

Also — I have a note saved that you prefer finance-related materials sent to vince2000@gmail.com. Do you want me to prepare the materials and email them to Vince once they’re ready?

What would you like me to do next — (A) run scenario estimates now using assumptions, (B) compute exact CAC/LTV if you provide the missing inputs, or (C) draft the one-page finance narrative and slide deck for review?

